## 🧰 Overview

This post documents my custom Pi5 laptop build, including enclosure design, cooling setup, and GPIO access. It’s part of my portfolio showcasing hardware modeling and prototyping routines.

## 🔧 Enclosure Design

The enclosure was modeled in Fusion 360 using metric units. Key features include:

- Snap-fit lid with screw mounts
- Ventilation slots aligned with the Pi5 fan
- GPIO breakout access on the side

![Enclosure Top View](enclosure-top.jpg){: .shadow }

## 🌬️ Cooling Setup

To prevent thermal throttling, I mounted a 30mm fan directly above the CPU using standoffs.

![Cooling Fan Mounted](cooling.jpg){: .normal }

## 🔌 GPIO Access

I routed GPIO pins to a side breakout using a ribbon cable and custom header.

![GPIO Breakout](gpio.jpg){: .wide }

## 📦 BOM & Specs

| Component        | Spec              | Source         |
|------------------|-------------------|----------------|
| Raspberry Pi 5   | 8GB RAM           | PiHut          |
| Fan              | 30mm, 5V          | AliExpress     |
| Screws           | M2.5 x 6mm        | Local hardware |
| Enclosure        | PLA, 3D printed   | Self-made      |

## 🧠 Lessons Learned

- Always validate airflow paths in CAD before printing
- GPIO clearance needs to be tested with real headers
- Documenting specs early helps with BOM clarity

